  "strategy": "This solution defines a hybrid optimization strategy that combines established step functions with a novel approach of incorporating \"dq\" components (representing derivative-like contributions) from the original convolution process. The algorithm evolves the sequence by mixing step contributions (quantities at indices that move through time) with derivative information from binding peaks at nearby time intervals. This method better models the security constraints of the evaluation function while improving the gradient quality.\n\nKey steps and strategy:\n1. Start by initializing the sequence from the parent if available, otherwise begin with a carefully constructed length-normalized Hann window.\n\n2. The main optimization loop:\n   - Periodically compute the top peaks of the convolution to identify critical indices.\n   - Use these peaks to compute directional contributions (dq) as gradients.\n   - Blend these dq components with the original step-based approach.\n   - Add noise-aligned evolution steps that spread updates across multiple influencing indices.\n\n3. Two critical helper functions:\n   - `get_peak_info`: Uses weighted centroid priority to identify critical peaks and their covariances.\n   - `get_direction_components`: Helps compute the dq values for scaling.\n\nThe implementation details include:\n- Limited to one phase due to performance constraints.\n- Initializing with appropriate noise dimensions and gradient updates (0.001 step).\n- Using uniform peak picking (100 top picks per index).\n- Random reconstruction phases (10 checks per stage) to model variability.\n\nThis strategy improves over the parent by mathematically better approximating how changes propagate through the convolution, allowing for more rapid convergence within the 1500-second timeframe while respecting all evaluation function constraints.",
